th='True friendship can grow between very different beings (an elephant and a dog); a prayer to be a caring, accepting friend',
sum='Gajaraj, the king’s elephant, lived in the best booth of the royal stables, but he was sad because he had no friends; the kind mahout was only his caretaker. One evening a hungry stray dog came in. Gajaraj pushed some of his food towards him; the dog ate and slept there. The mahout did not mind and gave the dog crumbs too. The dog went with Gajaraj to the pond, where the elephant playfully splashed water on him, and rode on his back on the way home. A farmer passing by recognised his lost dog, Buntee, tied a rope round his neck and took him home. Both friends were in tears. Gajaraj stopped eating; so did Buntee at the farmer’s house. The kind farmer understood that Buntee missed his friend and set him free. Buntee ran to the stable; Gajaraj swung him joyfully with his trunk. Both ate happily, and the mahout and the farmer also became friends. In the poem “A Friend’s Prayer”, Jill Wolf prays that friendships always be the most important thing to her; she feels blessed with special friends, wants to give her very best, help make their wishes come true, use her heart to understand them, make no judgements and love her friends the way they are.',
